{"items":[{"tags":["python","pandas","matplotlib","plot"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":74789406,"is_answered":false,"question_id":74789349,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1670956053,"creation_date":1670956053,"body":"You invoked in this sequence:\n\nPrefer this order:\n\n\nAlso, ignoring  warnings is ill advised.\nGo to the trouble of understanding what is happening,\nand fix the root cause.\nThe warning is there for good reason: to save you endless grief\ncaused by hard-to-diagnose bugs.\n","excerpt":"Also, ignoring <span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span> warnings is ill advised.\nGo to the trouble of understanding what is happening,\nand fix the root cause. &hellip; ","title":"Why does first plot in for loop not get formatted, while the rest are?"},{"tags":["python","pandas","pycharm"],"question_score":13,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":55565094,"item_type":"question","score":13,"last_activity_date":1663134738,"creation_date":1554686030,"body":"Looking to create my Pandas startup options for any project in Pycharm. I have a project called Test which has three modules.\nI created  with the settings I require, then created  so it would be loaded when Test project starts up. When I run on some test data, in  it fails as I&#39;m getting  which implies  never ran.\nThe second option was placing Startup_Panda_Options.py into folder  but the same issue arises.\nThird option, in PyCharm via File | Settings | Tools | Startup Tasks | I referenced the  file.\nSo three attempts based on suggestions from here and the documentation. Any ideas on how to get any of these options working?\nThe three modules are below:\n\n","excerpt":"If ‘truncate’ is specified, only print out the dimensions if the frame is truncated (e.g. not display all rows and/or columns)\n            },\n            &#39;mode&#39;: {\n                &#39;<span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span>&#39;: &hellip; ","title":"Set default Pandas options at Startup in PyCharm"},{"tags":["python","python-3.x"],"question_score":62,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":36346517,"is_answered":false,"question_id":7601823,"item_type":"answer","score":101,"last_activity_date":1648488211,"creation_date":1459473852,"body":"Left first\n\nis equivalent to\n\nNote the order. The leftmost target is assigned first.  (A similar expression in C may assign in the opposite order.) From the docs on Python assignment:\n\n...assigns the single resulting object to each of the target lists, from left to right.\n\nDisassembly shows this:\n\n\nCAUTION: the same object is always assigned to each target.  So as @Wilduck and @andronikus point out, you probably never want this:\n\nIn the above case x and y refer to the same list. Because lists are mutable, appending to x would seem to affect y.\n\nNow you have two names referring to two distinct empty lists.\n","excerpt":"Disassembly shows this:\n&gt;&gt;&gt; def <span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span>():\n... &hellip; x = y = some_function()\n...\n&gt;&gt;&gt; import dis\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dis.dis(<span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span>)\n  2           0 LOAD_GLOBAL              0 (some_function)\n              3 CALL_FUNCTION            0\n              6 DUP_TOP &hellip; ","title":"How do chained assignments work?"},{"tags":["pandas","keyerror"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":66838898,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1618416805,"creation_date":1616912519,"body":"I am trying to do a simple dataframe on pybaseball package. This was all working until this week 28/3/2021.  But now I am getting the following error:\n\nHere is the code that I have run:\n\nThis is being run in google colab.\nAny assistance appreciated\n","excerpt":"not None:\n\nKeyError: &#39;Zone% (pfx)&#39;\n\nHere is the code that I have run:\nimport pandas as pd\n\npd.set_option(&#39;display.max_rows&#39;, None)\n\npd.set_option(&#39;display.max_columns&#39;, None)\n\npd.set_option(&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span> &hellip; ","title":"How to fix this error in pandas datafrom - KeyError: &#39;Zone% (pfx)&#39;"},{"tags":["python","pandas","k-means","pca"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":67002289,"is_answered":false,"question_id":67001411,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1617878417,"creation_date":1617878417,"body":"\n","excerpt":"import pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\nfrom sklearn.cluster import KMeans\npd.set_option(&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span>&#39;, None)\nnp.random.seed(100)\n\n# define the input data frame &hellip; ","title":"How to buld a loop for running PCA and K-means for different segment in pandas"},{"tags":["python","pandas","dataframe","nan"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":64268002,"is_answered":false,"question_id":64267854,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1602205605,"creation_date":1602179178,"body":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAnother solution\n\n","excerpt":"df = pd.DataFrame({&#39;a&#39;: a, &#39;b&#39;: b})\ndf.at[[0, 4], &#39;a&#39;] = None\n\ndf\n\n\n    a   b\n0   NaN 0\n1   1   1\n2   2   4\n3   3   9\n4   NaN 16\n5   5   25\n\n\n# to avoid the assignment warning\npd.set_option(&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span> &hellip; ","title":"How to conditionally update a dataframe, from another dataframe"},{"tags":["python","pandas","error-handling"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":57772540,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1567516481,"creation_date":1567516481,"body":"I want to suppress warnings from pandas just after import this way:\n\n1)  -- returns option error: no such keys(s): &#39;chained_assigment&#39;\n\n2)  -- return you can only set the value of existing options\n\nHow to set this param?\n","excerpt":"I want to suppress warnings from pandas just after import this way:\n\n1) pd.set_option(&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span>&#39;,None) -- returns option error: no such keys(s): &#39;chained_assigment&#39;\n\n2) pandas.options.mode.chained_assignment &hellip; ","title":"OptionError while trying to set chained_assigment to None in pandas"},{"tags":["python-3.x","pandas","dataframe","artificial-intelligence"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":57626617,"is_answered":false,"question_id":57605863,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1566564941,"creation_date":1566564295,"body":"Initially I was hoping to reduce the size of my .csv file by cutting down the unnecessary data before entering it into the code. However for my files I found that the memory or the speed would not be an issue. And since the cutting beforehand approach was giving me too many errors, I tried a different method. \n\nSolved this furthering on the &quot;idea&quot; described in the question. Posting code with details below. It is perhaps a longer approach, but for a novice like me, it solves the job. \n\n\n\nAnd finally\n\n\n\n\n\nThis was the final result. The Unnamed (n) columns are the  columns from the example data in the question. Apparently the columns with the titles included the  columns. \n\nThe data I had was quite large, several hundred columns and rows. By default pandas dataframe has a set value of rows and columns in the window it shows. To alter these values to fit your dataset, the following code can be used:\n\n\n\nHope this helps anyone looking for the solution. \nThe data isn&#39;t mine to post on a public forum, hence I apologize for not being able to share more details. \n","excerpt":"max_rows&#39;: 85,\n            &#39;max_seq_items&#39;: 50,         # Max length of printed sequence\n            &#39;precision&#39;: 4,\n            &#39;show_dimensions&#39;: False\n        },\n        &#39;mode&#39;: {\n            &#39;<span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span> &hellip; ","title":"Extracting dataframe columns for mapped data"},{"tags":["python-3.x","pandas"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":55543871,"is_answered":false,"question_id":55541957,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1554501481,"creation_date":1554501481,"body":"This is a chained assignment issue.  That may be manipulated by .\n\nThe warnings are turned on, and Pandas does not like .  \n\nThere are a couple options:  Make sure  is the source dataframe you intend to modify (which you are doing by placing a new column named  in it).  If it is, and Pandas is wrong, set . Can Pandas be wrong about that? I don&#39;t know.\n\nHere is an illustration of setting a value on a slice.\n\n\n\nResults\n\n\n","excerpt":"That may be manipulated by pd.set_option(&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span>&#39;,None|&#39;warn&#39;|&#39;raise&#39;).\n\nThe warnings are turned on, and Pandas does not like coll_train. &hellip; If it is, and Pandas is wrong, set pd.set_option(&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span>&#39;,None). Can Pandas be wrong about that? I don&#39;t know.\n\nHere is an illustration of setting a value on a slice. &hellip; ","title":"Making a copy of a object pandas column as a category type giving warning"},{"tags":["python","function","dataframe"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":54498148,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1549150740,"creation_date":1549146647,"body":"I have a python script that organizes data from site regarding NHL players. The values are being placed into a dataframe. I also have built a function that takes players names and team name to get the total salary of the players line up. I want to pass the players names (F1, F2, F3) from the dataframe to the function (names) and store the result in my excel sheet (totalSalary).  \n\nI&#39;ve tried iloc function to pass into function and I&#39;m getting confused.\n\n\n","excerpt":"50,         # Max length of printed sequence\n            &#39;precision&#39;: 4,\n            &#39;show_dimensions&#39;: False,\n            &#39;colheader_justify&#39;: &#39;left&#39;\n        },\n        &#39;mode&#39;: {\n            &#39;<span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span> &hellip; ","title":"How do I pass dataframe column values to my custom function to store total amount?"},{"tags":["python","pandas","chained-assignment"],"question_score":4,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":47665332,"is_answered":false,"question_id":37444221,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1512527744,"creation_date":1512523143,"body":"Chained assignment will now by default warn if the user is assigning to a copy.\n\nThis can be changed with the option mode.chained_assignment, allowed options are raise/warn/None. See the docs.\n\nIn [5]: dfc = DataFrame({&#39;A&#39;:[&#39;aaa&#39;,&#39;bbb&#39;,&#39;ccc&#39;],&#39;B&#39;:[1,2,3]})\n\nIn [6]: pd.set_option(&#39;chained_assignment&#39;,&#39;warn&#39;)\n\nThe following warning / exception will show if this is attempted.\n\nIn [7]: dfc.loc[0][&#39;A&#39;] = 1111\n\nTraceback (most recent call last)\n   ...\nSettingWithCopyWarning:\n   A value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\n   Try using .loc[row_index,col_indexer] = value instead\nHere is the correct method of assignment.\n\nIn [8]: dfc.loc[0,&#39;A&#39;] = 11\n\nIn [9]: dfc\n\n\n\n0    11  1\n\n1   bbb  2\n\n2   ccc  3\n","excerpt":"In [5]: dfc = DataFrame({&#39;A&#39;:[&#39;aaa&#39;,&#39;bbb&#39;,&#39;ccc&#39;],&#39;B&#39;:[1,2,3]})\n\nIn [6]: pd.set_option(&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span>&#39;,&#39;warn&#39;)\n\nThe following warning / exception will show if this is attempted. &hellip; ","title":"Python: SettingWithCopyWarning: A value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame"},{"tags":["python","pandas","chained-assignment"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":47597204,"is_answered":false,"question_id":47597022,"item_type":"answer","score":2,"last_activity_date":1512144718,"creation_date":1512144553,"body":"\n  I&#39;d like to fetch a Series and make changes to it, which I&#39;d like\n  reflected in the DataFrame later on.\n\n\nIn this case, you should temporarily disable this warning and proceed as you are now.  Using  will mean your original  will be unmoidified by changes to .\n\n\n\n is a context manager, meaning it can be used with , and the option only applies to code within the block.\n\nRead more: pandas &gt; Getting &amp; Setting Options\n","excerpt":",[4,5,6]], columns=list(&#39;abc&#39;))\n    df[&#39;d&#39;] = df[&#39;a&#39;].diff()\n    d = df.loc[:, &#39;d&#39;]\n    d.loc[d&gt;0] *= 3\n\n# Code you run outside of `with` will maintain your original setting:\n# pd.get_option(&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span> &hellip; ","title":"Can&#39;t get around Pandas Series SettingWithCopyWarning"},{"tags":["python","pandas","exception"],"question_score":12,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":47182183,"item_type":"question","score":12,"last_activity_date":1510156273,"creation_date":1510150991,"body":"If you worked in python with pandas you already know the chained_assignment warning when working on slices of dataframes (as e.g. described here).\n\nI found the option  which can be set to \n\n\n, ignoring the warning\n, printing a warning message\n, raising an exception\n\n\ncompare with documentation\n\nI included a minimal example, triggering this warning within a  block for exception handling. I expect an exception to be triggered only with the setting , as shown in Example 3 below.\n\nIn this minimal example, the behaviour is as expected, so that Example 2, with \n only causes a warning message to be printed, but no exception is raised.\n\nHowever in a larger framework, I see an exception being raised even though the parameter is set to , checked with a print before like in the minimal example (see Example 4)\n\nIs there any other pandas parameter influencing the behavior of this warning message concerning exception raising?\n\n\n\nHere is a minimal example of code, setting/printing the  parameter and showing the behaviour in a  block.\n\n\n\n\n\nExample 1\nSetting  results in the following output (no warning, no exception)\n\n\n\n\n\nExample 2\nSetting  results in the following output (a warning is printed, but no exception)\n\n\n\n\n\nExample 3\nSetting  results in the following output (an exception is raised)\n\n\n\n\n\nExample 4\nThis is what I see in a larger framework with exactly the same test code. I do not set the chained  parameter explictly, but I see it&#39;s set to , even though an exception is raised\n\n\n","excerpt":"If you worked in python with pandas you already know the <span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span> warning when working on slices of dataframes (as e.g. described here). &hellip; import pandas as pd\n\n# set the <span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span> option\npd.options.mode.chained_assignment = &quot;raise&quot; # raises exception in case of warning\npd.options.mode.chained_assignment = &quot;warn&quot;  # prints warning &hellip; ","title":"pandas chained_assignment warning exception handling"},{"tags":["python","pandas"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":27350022,"is_answered":false,"question_id":27349552,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1418002714,"creation_date":1418001828,"body":"\n  \n  Should I really care about the warning?\n  \n\n\nIt depends. In your example you are first referring to a subset of the data() and then setting values on this subset. Almost always pandas makes a  of the original data and setting values on the copy will not modify the original Dataframe, hence the warning. \n\nIn some cases pandas will make a  of the original data(ex: if all columns have the same dtype.), setting values here will work as expected.\n\nIf all columns in your dataframe have the same dtype(ex: all floats) and you are using  on a single existing index then you are getting a view and the warning can be ignored. If you are setting on a non-existing index the you are Setting with enlargment, this is also the expected behaviour and the warning can also be ignored.\n\n\n  \n  What is the recommended way to go about this so that the warning doesn&#39;t pop up?\n  \n\n\nYour use of  given the info you provided seems alright. You have several alternatives to avoid the warning: \n\nFirst, update your version of pandas. The situation with these false positive warnings has been improving with each version, I don&#39;t get any in 0.15.1. \n\nSecond, If you are sure that what you are doing is the intended behaviour then you can just silence the warning globally with:\n\n\n\nFinally, in some cases you can set the  property of your resulting object effectively disabling the checks on this object, for example:\n\n\n\nNote that this last option can only be used on existing indexes, on new indexes this raises a .\n","excerpt":"Second, If you are sure that what you are doing is the intended behaviour then you can just silence the warning globally with:\n\npd.set_option(&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span>&#39;, None)\n\n\nFinally, in some cases you can &hellip; ","title":"adding row to pandas dataframe from series"},{"tags":["python","pandas"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":27204280,"is_answered":false,"question_id":27204059,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1417279327,"creation_date":1417277752,"body":"Just do it like this:\n\n\n\nor even:\n\n\n\nRegarding your question: I think this is a false warning in this particular case, as  should return a view and not a copy. You can turn off the warning with:\n\n\n","excerpt":"You can turn off the warning with:\n\npd.set_option(&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span>&#39;, None) &hellip; ","title":"Pandas dataframe column calculation"},{"tags":["python","pandas"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":24063660,"is_answered":false,"question_id":24063623,"item_type":"answer","score":2,"last_activity_date":1401980501,"creation_date":1401980501,"body":"Pandas is limiting the number of rows to display, you can change this:\n\n\n\nwould fix that, there is a little about this online\n\nalternatively you can either print the dataframe:  or just display the top  rows:\n\n\n\nIf you are in IPython then tab-completion will display the default and current settings:\n\n\n  Available options:\n  \n  \n  display.[chop_threshold, colheader_justify, column_space, date_dayfirst,   date_yearfirst, encoding, expand_frame_repr,\n  float_format, height, large_repr,   line_width, max_columns,\n  max_colwidth, max_info_columns, max_info_rows,   max_rows,\n  max_seq_items, mpl_style, multi_sparse, notebook_repr_html,\n  pprint_nest_depth, precision, show_dimensions, width]\n  io.excel.xls.[writer]\n  io.excel.xlsm.[writer]\n  io.excel.xlsx.[writer]\n  io.hdf.[default_format, dropna_table]\n  mode.[chained_assignment, sim_interactive, use_inf_as_null]\n  \n  \n  Parameters\n  ---------- pat : str\n      Regexp which should match a single option.\n      Note: partial matches are supported for convenience, but unless you use the\n      full option name (e.g. x.y.z.option_name), your code may break in future\n      versions if new options with similar names are introduced. value :\n      new value of option.\n  \n  Returns\n  ------- None\n  \n  Raises\n  ------ OptionError if no such option exists\n  \n  Notes\n  ----- The available options with its descriptions:\n  \n  display.line_width : [default: 80] [currently: 80]: int\n          Deprecated.\n          (Deprecated, use  instead.)\n  \n  display.max_columns : [default: 20] [currently: 20]: int\n          max_rows and max_columns are used in repr() methods to decide if\n          to_string() or info() is used to render an object to a string.  In case\n          python/IPython is running in a terminal this can be set to 0 and pandas\n          will correctly auto-detect the width the terminal and swap to a smaller\n          format in case all columns would not fit vertically. The IPython notebook,\n          IPython qtconsole, or IDLE do not run in a terminal and hence it is not\n          possible to do correct auto-detection.\n          &#39;None&#39; value means unlimited.\n  \n  display.max_colwidth : [default: 50] [currently: 50]: int\n          The maximum width in characters of a column in the repr of\n          a pandas data structure. When the column overflows, a &quot;...&quot;\n          placeholder is embedded in the output.\n  \n  display.max_info_columns : [default: 100] [currently: 100]: int\n          max_info_columns is used in DataFrame.info method to decide if\n          per column information will be printed.\n  \n  display.max_info_rows : [default: 1690785] [currently: 1690785]: int\n  or None\n          df.info() will usually show null-counts for each column.\n          For large frames this can be quite slow. max_info_rows and max_info_cols\n          limit this null check only to frames with smaller dimensions then specified.\n  \n  display.max_rows : [default: 60] [currently: 60]: int\n          This sets the maximum number of rows pandas should output when printing\n          out various output. For example, this value determines whether the repr()\n          for a dataframe prints out fully or just a summary repr.\n          &#39;None&#39; value means unlimited.\n\n\nI&#39;ve trimmed the output for brevity\n","excerpt":"[<span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span>, sim_interactive, use_inf_as_null]\n  \n  \n  Parameters\n  ---------- pat : str\n      Regexp which should match a single option. &hellip; ","title":"Pandas read DataFrame"},{"tags":["python","pandas","copy","chained-assignment"],"question_score":19,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":21463854,"is_answered":false,"question_id":21463589,"item_type":"answer","score":31,"last_activity_date":1391111388,"creation_date":1391104189,"body":"The point of the  is to warn the user that you may be doing something that will not update the original data frame as one might expect.\n\nHere,  is a dataframe, possibly of a single dtype (or not). You are then taking a reference to this  which is a Series, and updating it. This probably works in your case because you are returning the same dtype of data as existed.\n\nHowever it could create a copy which updates a copy of  which you would not see; Then you would be wondering why it is not updating.\n\nPandas returns a copy of an object in almost all method calls. The  operations are a convience operation which work, but in general are not clear that data is being modified and could potentially work on copies.\n\nMuch more clear to do this:\n\n\n\nOne further plus to working on copies. You can chain operations, this is not possible with  ones.\n\ne.g.\n\n\n\nAnd just an FYI.  operations are neither faster nor more memory efficient. my2c they should be banned. But too late on that API.\n\nYou can of course turn this off:\n\n\n\nPandas runs with the entire test suite with this set to  (so we know if chaining is happening) on, FYI.\n","excerpt":"You can of course turn this off:\n\npd.set_option(&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">chained_assignment</span>&#39;,None)\n\n\nPandas runs with the entire test suite with this set to raise (so we know if chaining is happening) on, FYI. &hellip; ","title":"Pandas: Chained assignments"}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":231}